# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CREDENTIALS — never commit. This repo is public.
#
# The Yahoo OAuth libraries persist a LIVE REFRESH TOKEN to a JSON file in the
# working directory (yahoo_oauth writes oauth2.json by default). A refresh token
# is an account credential: it can be exchanged for access tokens until it is
# revoked or the account password changes. Treat a leak as an account
# compromise, not a config mistake.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
oauth2.json
*token*.json
private.json
secrets.json
credentials.json
*.pem
*.key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Working notes that describe this project more completely than the code does.
#
# The repo is public, and stays public while the Yahoo API application is under
# review. The handover is the single most revealing file in it: 3,000+ lines of
# current state, open questions, what is stubbed, who plays in the league, and
# what is worth trying next. None of it is secret and all of it is interesting,
# which is the problem — it is the difference between a public repo that reads
# as a scoring library and one that reads as a family's league.
#
# The file itself is NOT deleted. It stays at docs/handover.md, untracked, and
# every session still reads and refreshes it. This only stops it being
# published from here on.
#
# Note what this does not do: docs/handover.md is already in this repo's history
# and in the diffs of merged PRs, both of which stay readable while it is
# public. This closes the tap; it does not drain the tank.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs/handover.md

